  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the dining room, Vika meets with influential official Nikolai Maryanovich to arrange a last-minute birthday banquet. Max interrupts them, furious that Vika did not tell him her contractions were false.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я хочу закрыть ресторан на спецобслуживание.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Свой день рождения буду отмечать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Конечно, а когда именно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Ну, сегодня или завтра вечером.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Аренда ресторана заранее обговаривается.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вика, можно тебя на минуту?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Подожди, я сейчас разговариваю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А почему ты не в роддоме?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Утренние схватки оказались ложными.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Меня врачи домой отпустили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А позвонить мне нельзя было?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Юноша, подождите, пока мы закончим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я просто поговорю с женой!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я хочу закрыть ресторан на спецобслуживание.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want to close the restaurant for a private event.' the guest brings out the contrast between 'I' and 'want' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Свой день рождения буду отмечать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will be celebrating my birthday.' the guest anchors the line on 'my own', while 'celebrate' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Конечно, а когда именно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Of course, and when exactly?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'of course' and finishing on 'specifically'. the guest answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Ну, сегодня или завтра вечером.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, tonight or tomorrow evening.' The meaning hinges on 'come on' working together with 'in the evening' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Аренда ресторана заранее обговаривается.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Renting the restaurant is arranged in advance.' The grammar packs 'rental' and 'is arranged' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вика, можно тебя на минуту?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vika, can I have a word with you?' Max anchors the line on 'Vika', while 'a minute' completes the thought. Vika answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Подожди, я сейчас разговариваю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, I am talking right now.' Notice how Russian expresses 'wait' and 'to talk' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А почему ты не в роддоме?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And why are you not at the maternity hospital?' Max anchors the line on 'and', while 'maternity hospital' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Утренние схватки оказались ложными.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The morning contractions turned out to be false.' The grammar packs 'morning' and 'false' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Меня врачи домой отпустили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The doctors let me go home.' Vika brings out the contrast between 'me' and 'doctor' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А позвонить мне нельзя было?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Could you not have called me?' In Russian, the idea of 'and' flows naturally into 'was' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Юноша, подождите, пока мы закончим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Young man, wait until we finish.' The meaning hinges on 'young man' working together with 'finish' in spoken Russian. Max answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я просто поговорю с женой!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will just talk to my wife!' Max anchors the line on 'I', while 'wife' completes the thought. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
